What a delta chain costs to read back cold, and what read concurrency is worth.

host:     AWS i4i.metal, 128 logical cpus, real in-region S3
build:    this branch (concurrent hydration across chunks and across a chunk's
          layers); "serial" figures for comparison are from
          raw/metal-concurrent-upload-s3.log, same setup
date_utc: 2026-07-27

blockd/tests/commit_cost.rs::cold_read_cost_by_delta_chain_length. Each of 16
chunks is written once densely (a base) and then `layers` more times, each
touching a different page, so the chain really is that deep. The local cache is
then deleted and all 64 MiB is read back -- what a cross-node migration or a fresh
host does.

Two read shapes, because they behave differently:
  chunk-at-a-time -- one 4 MiB read per call, what a Guest produces. Only the
                     layers within a chunk can overlap.
  one big read    -- the whole volume in one call, what a migration produces.
                     Chunks overlap as well.

--- real S3 --------------------------------------------------------------------

 layers          objects chunk-at-a-time ms        MiB/s one big read ms        MiB/s
      0               16          840.5           76          155.4          412
      1               32          852.0           75          154.7          414
      2               48          916.9           70          165.8          386
      4               80          857.7           75          171.9          372

--- control: local file store, same machine ------------------------------------

      0               16           81.8          783           52.0         1232
      1               32           80.4          796           52.7         1214
      2               48           81.1          789           51.9         1234
      4               80           82.0          781           53.1         1206

Reading:

* **A full delta chain is nearly free to read.** Four layers is five objects per
  chunk -- 80 fetches instead of 16 -- and costs 171.9 ms against 155.4 ms, about
  **11%**. ADR-0016 charges cold-read amplification as its main cost and sized
  MAX_DELTA_LAYERS around it; measured, with the layers fetched together, that
  cost is small. The bound still matters as a bound, but it is not the thing to
  optimize next.
* **Overlapping chunks is worth 5.4x**: 155 ms against 840 ms for the same 64 MiB,
  412 MiB/s against 76. A single-chunk read cannot overlap anything by definition,
  so a Guest reading 4 MiB at a time still sees ~76 MiB/s per outstanding request
  and gets its parallelism from its own queue depth; a migration reading in bulk
  gets it from here.
* 412 MiB/s is a single volume against S3 and is not the instance's ceiling. The
  next thing in the way is per-object GET latency at this concurrency, not the
  chain length.

